1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,787
This is MATH 239, video 18a. It works through problems 1 to 5 of the Practice Midterm.

2
00:00:08,237 --> 00:00:11,970
After this video, you can do the five things in this list.

3
00:00:12,420 --> 00:00:16,537
The Practice Midterm has 10 problems, Q1 to Q10.

4
00:00:16,987 --> 00:00:22,427
Each bar shows the marks of one problem. Together they are worth 79 marks.

5
00:00:22,877 --> 00:00:30,579
The real midterm has about 50 marks. The practice paper has more problems, so that it covers all topics.

6
00:00:31,029 --> 00:00:38,879
This video does problems 1 to 5. They use chapters 1 and 2 of the notes. Their topics are next to the bars.

7
00:00:39,329 --> 00:00:42,487
Video 18b does problems 6 to 10.

8
00:00:42,937 --> 00:00:50,702
You can use all results from class without proof. Name each result that you use, for example the Product Lemma.

9
00:00:51,152 --> 00:00:53,179
And justify every answer.

10
00:00:53,629 --> 00:00:58,471
Before each solution, pause the video and try the problem on paper first.

11
00:00:58,921 --> 00:01:01,012
Problem 1 is worth 10 marks.

12
00:01:01,462 --> 00:01:05,665
Let k and n be integers, with k between 0 and n.

13
00:01:06,115 --> 00:01:14,157
S is the set of ordered pairs A, B. A and B are subsets of 1 to n, and each has exactly k elements.

14
00:01:14,607 --> 00:01:19,727
Here is one element of S, for n equal to 4 and k equal to 2.

15
00:01:20,177 --> 00:01:21,799
A is the set 1, 2.

16
00:01:22,249 --> 00:01:24,147
B is the set 2, 4.

17
00:01:24,597 --> 00:01:28,565
A and B can share elements. Here they share the element 2.

18
00:01:29,015 --> 00:01:44,439
We must prove this identity. n choose k, squared, equals the sum over i from 0 to k of n choose i, times n minus i choose k minus i, times n minus k choose k minus i.

19
00:01:44,889 --> 00:01:48,239
The method is given: count S in two different ways.

20
00:01:48,689 --> 00:01:55,814
The hint says: look at the possible values of the size of A intersect B. Pause the video and try it.

21
00:01:56,264 --> 00:02:03,816
First count. Build a pair in two choices. Choose A first. A is a k-element subset of an n-element set.

22
00:02:04,266 --> 00:02:08,575
By Theorem 1.5, there are n choose k choices for A.

23
00:02:09,025 --> 00:02:15,212
For n equal to 4 and k equal to 2, these choices are the 6 rows of the grid.

24
00:02:15,662 --> 00:02:22,937
Then choose B. The choices for B do not depend on A. So B also has n choose k choices.

25
00:02:23,387 --> 00:02:28,293
For n equal to 4 and k equal to 2, these are the 6 columns.

26
00:02:28,743 --> 00:02:40,413
Each pair A, B is one cell of the grid. For example, the cell in row 1 2 and column 2 4 is the pair A equal to 1, 2 and B equal to 2, 4.

27
00:02:40,863 --> 00:02:47,369
The grid has 6 rows and 6 columns. So it has 6 times 6, which is 36, cells.

28
00:02:47,819 --> 00:02:57,611
In general, the size of S is n choose k, times n choose k. That is n choose k, squared. This is the left side of the identity.

29
00:02:58,061 --> 00:03:04,461
Second count. Let S sub i be the set of pairs in S with exactly i common elements.

30
00:03:04,911 --> 00:03:11,845
i can be any number from 0 to k. The sets S sub i are disjoint, and their union is S.

31
00:03:12,295 --> 00:03:22,556
Fix i. We build a pair in S sub i with three choices. The example has n equal to 7, k equal to 3, and i equal to 1.

32
00:03:23,006 --> 00:03:26,590
First, choose A intersect B. It has i elements.

33
00:03:27,040 --> 00:03:31,563
They come from all of 1 to n. So there are n choose i choices.

34
00:03:32,013 --> 00:03:37,261
In the example, the pool has all 7 elements. We choose the element 3.

35
00:03:37,711 --> 00:03:41,273
This choice has 7 choose 1, which is 7, ways.

36
00:03:41,723 --> 00:03:47,377
Second, choose the rest of A. That is A minus B. It has k minus i elements.

37
00:03:47,827 --> 00:03:56,765
They come from the n minus i elements that are not in A intersect B. So there are n minus i choose k minus i choices.

38
00:03:57,215 --> 00:04:04,447
In the example, 3 is used, so the pool has 6 elements. We choose 1 and 5. Now A is complete.

39
00:04:04,897 --> 00:04:08,396
This choice has 6 choose 2, which is 15, ways.

40
00:04:08,846 --> 00:04:13,539
Third, choose B minus A. It also has k minus i elements.

41
00:04:13,989 --> 00:04:23,547
They must not be in A. A has k elements, so n minus k elements are left. There are n minus k choose k minus i choices.

42
00:04:23,997 --> 00:04:32,487
In the example, A is 1, 3, 5. So the pool has 4 elements: 2, 4, 6 and 7. We choose 2 and 6.

43
00:04:32,937 --> 00:04:36,287
This choice has 4 choose 2, which is 6, ways.

44
00:04:36,737 --> 00:04:46,358
The three choices give each pair in S sub i exactly once. Here they give A equal to 1, 3, 5, and B equal to 2, 3, 6.

45
00:04:46,808 --> 00:04:52,248
So the size of S sub i is the product of the three binomial coefficients.

46
00:04:52,698 --> 00:04:58,757
In the example, the product is 7 times 15 times 6, which is 630.

47
00:04:59,207 --> 00:05:04,305
Add over i from 0 to k. This gives the right side of the identity.

48
00:05:04,755 --> 00:05:08,958
Both counts give the size of S. So the two sides are equal.

49
00:05:09,408 --> 00:05:19,563
Check the identity with n equal to 4 and k equal to 2. Each cell A, B of the grid now shows i, the size of A intersect B.

50
00:05:20,013 --> 00:05:25,581
i equal to 2 means that A equals B. These are the 6 cells on the diagonal.

51
00:05:26,031 --> 00:05:35,353
The formula gives 4 choose 2, times 2 choose 0, times 2 choose 0. That is 6 times 1 times 1, which is 6.

52
00:05:35,803 --> 00:05:42,161
i equal to 1 means that A and B share one element. These are the 24 cells with a 1.

53
00:05:42,611 --> 00:05:52,189
The formula gives 4 choose 1, times 3 choose 1, times 2 choose 1. That is 4 times 3 times 2, which is 24.

54
00:05:52,639 --> 00:05:58,869
i equal to 0 means that A and B are disjoint. These are the 6 cells with a 0.

55
00:05:59,319 --> 00:06:08,727
The formula gives 4 choose 0, times 4 choose 2, times 2 choose 2. That is 1 times 6 times 1, which is 6.

56
00:06:09,177 --> 00:06:16,665
6 plus 24 plus 6 is 36. That is 6 squared, so it equals 4 choose 2, squared.

57
00:06:17,115 --> 00:06:21,424
A python check confirms the identity for all n up to 7.

58
00:06:21,874 --> 00:06:36,914
Here is the written solution. Count 1: there are n choose k choices for A, and independently n choose k choices for B, by Theorem 1.5. So the size of S is n choose k, squared.

59
00:06:37,364 --> 00:06:44,276
Count 2: for each i from 0 to k, define S sub i, the pairs with i common elements.

60
00:06:44,726 --> 00:06:54,113
Write why the S sub i are disjoint and why their union is S. The size of A intersect B is always between 0 and k.

61
00:06:54,563 --> 00:06:57,997
Then give the three choices with their numbers of ways.

62
00:06:58,447 --> 00:07:05,807
Say that each pair arises exactly once. This sentence shows that the product is the size of S sub i.

63
00:07:06,257 --> 00:07:11,335
Add the sizes of the S sub i. This gives the size of S as the sum.

64
00:07:11,785 --> 00:07:18,377
Both counts give the size of S. So the two expressions are equal. End the proof with this sentence.

65
00:07:18,827 --> 00:07:31,904
A common mistake is in the third factor. Some students choose B minus A from the n minus i elements outside A intersect B. That gives n minus i choose k minus i.

66
00:07:32,354 --> 00:07:39,821
Take the example n equal to 7, k equal to 3, i equal to 1, and A equal to 1, 3, 5.

67
00:07:40,271 --> 00:07:46,820
The wrong pool has the 6 elements other than 3. It still contains 1 and 5, which are in A.

68
00:07:47,270 --> 00:07:49,830
Then B can be the set 1, 3, 6.

69
00:07:50,280 --> 00:07:58,899
Now A intersect B is the set 1, 3. It has 2 elements, not i equal to 1. So this pair is not in S sub 1.

70
00:07:59,349 --> 00:08:05,087
The pool for B minus A must avoid all of A. So it has n minus k elements.

71
00:08:05,537 --> 00:08:13,388
Also, some terms of the sum are 0. Take n equal to 5, k equal to 3, and i equal to 0.

72
00:08:13,838 --> 00:08:26,681
A takes 3 of the 5 elements. Only n minus k, which is 5 minus 3, which is 2, elements are left. But B minus A needs k minus i, which is 3, elements.

73
00:08:27,131 --> 00:08:30,096
So the term has 2 choose 3, which is 0.

74
00:08:30,546 --> 00:08:40,786
Two 3-element subsets of a 5-element set always share an element. The formula gives 0 for this case, so no special case is necessary.

75
00:08:41,236 --> 00:08:45,204
Problem 2 is worth 10 marks. Let n be at least 3.

76
00:08:45,654 --> 00:08:49,729
A sub n is the set of 3-element subsets of 1 to n.

77
00:08:50,179 --> 00:08:54,552
For n equal to 5, there are 10 such subsets. Here they are.

78
00:08:55,002 --> 00:09:02,746
B sub n is the set of triples b 1, b 2, b 3 of positive integers whose sum is at most n.

79
00:09:03,196 --> 00:09:07,228
For n equal to 5, these are the 10 triples on the right.

80
00:09:07,678 --> 00:09:09,598
Both sets have 10 elements.

81
00:09:10,048 --> 00:09:17,664
We must define a bijection f from A sub n to B sub n, and show where f sends each element of A sub 5.

82
00:09:18,114 --> 00:09:21,165
Then show that f of S is in B sub n.

83
00:09:21,615 --> 00:09:25,561
And state the inverse. Other details are not necessary.

84
00:09:26,011 --> 00:09:27,654
Pause the video and try it.

85
00:09:28,104 --> 00:09:33,011
Write the elements of S in increasing order: a less than b less than c.

86
00:09:33,461 --> 00:09:41,034
In the example, n is 9, and S is the set 2, 5, 6. The orange cells are the elements of S.

87
00:09:41,484 --> 00:09:45,452
Cut the row 1 to n after a, after b, and after c.

88
00:09:45,902 --> 00:09:52,174
The first piece is 1 to a. It has a cells. Let b 1 equal a. Here b 1 is 2.

89
00:09:52,624 --> 00:10:02,736
The second piece is a plus 1 to b. It has b minus a cells. Let b 2 equal b minus a. Here b 2 is 5 minus 2, which is 3.

90
00:10:03,186 --> 00:10:14,322
The third piece is b plus 1 to c. It has c minus b cells. Let b 3 equal c minus b. Here b 3 is 6 minus 5, which is 1.

91
00:10:14,772 --> 00:10:19,913
Each piece ends at an element of S. So each piece has at least one cell.

92
00:10:20,363 --> 00:10:23,905
The rest of the row, c plus 1 to n, is not used.

93
00:10:24,355 --> 00:10:34,296
So b 1 plus b 2 plus b 3 equals c, which is at most n. Here 2 plus 3 plus 1 is 6, and 6 is at most 9.

94
00:10:34,746 --> 00:10:39,695
Define f of a, b, c as the triple a, b minus a, c minus b.

95
00:10:40,145 --> 00:10:43,665
For the set 2, 5, 6, f gives 2, 3, 1.

96
00:10:44,115 --> 00:10:51,326
Now apply f to the 10 elements of A sub 5. The definition of f is in the green box at the top.

97
00:10:51,776 --> 00:11:00,416
1, 2, 3 goes to 1, 1, 1. 1, 2, 4 goes to 1, 1, 2. 1, 2, 5 goes to 1, 1, 3.

98
00:11:00,866 --> 00:11:09,762
1, 3, 4 goes to 1, 2, 1. 1, 3, 5 goes to 1, 2, 2. 1, 4, 5 goes to 1, 3, 1.

99
00:11:10,212 --> 00:11:18,745
2, 3, 4 goes to 2, 1, 1. 2, 3, 5 goes to 2, 1, 2. 2, 4, 5 goes to 2, 2, 1.

100
00:11:19,195 --> 00:11:22,225
And 3, 4, 5 goes to 3, 1, 1.

101
00:11:22,675 --> 00:11:30,056
Each triple of B sub 5 gets exactly one arrow. So for n equal to 5, f is a bijection.

102
00:11:30,506 --> 00:11:35,071
The question asks for this table. So write all 10 lines in the exam.

103
00:11:35,521 --> 00:11:40,535
We must show that f of S is in B sub n, for every S in A sub n.

104
00:11:40,985 --> 00:11:47,235
Let S be a, b, c, with 1 at most a, a less than b, b less than c, and c at most n.

105
00:11:47,685 --> 00:11:52,379
b 1 equals a. Since a is at least 1, b 1 is at least 1.

106
00:11:52,829 --> 00:12:00,530
b 2 equals b minus a. b and a are integers, and b is larger than a. So b 2 is at least 1.

107
00:12:00,980 --> 00:12:05,865
In the same way, b 3 equals c minus b, and it is at least 1.

108
00:12:06,315 --> 00:12:15,339
The sum b 1 plus b 2 plus b 3 is a, plus b minus a, plus c minus b. The terms cancel, and the sum is c.

109
00:12:15,789 --> 00:12:21,315
And c is at most n. All conditions hold, so f of S is in B sub n.

110
00:12:21,765 --> 00:12:30,298
The inverse undoes the cuts. Take a triple b 1, b 2, b 3 in B sub n. Here the triple is 1, 3, 1.

111
00:12:30,748 --> 00:12:35,313
Put down blocks of lengths b 1, b 2 and b 3, from the left.

112
00:12:35,763 --> 00:12:42,718
The blocks end at b 1, at b 1 plus b 2, and at b 1 plus b 2 plus b 3.

113
00:12:43,168 --> 00:12:47,840
Define f inverse of the triple as the set of these three numbers.

114
00:12:48,290 --> 00:12:55,885
For 1, 3, 1, we get 1, 1 plus 3, and 1 plus 3 plus 1. That is the set 1, 4, 5.

115
00:12:56,335 --> 00:13:06,937
These are three different numbers, because each b is at least 1. The smallest is at least 1, and the largest is at most n. So the set is in A sub n.

116
00:13:07,387 --> 00:13:12,721
Apply f to this set. The differences give back b 1, b 2 and b 3.

117
00:13:13,171 --> 00:13:22,280
So the two maps are inverses, and by Proposition 1.11 f is a bijection. The question does not ask for this check.

118
00:13:22,730 --> 00:13:37,514
Here is the written solution. First the definition: for S equal to a, b, c with a less than b less than c, f of S is a, b minus a, c minus b. Say that the elements are in increasing order.

119
00:13:37,964 --> 00:13:41,377
Then the table for A sub 5, with all 10 lines.

120
00:13:41,827 --> 00:13:50,958
Then the check. a is at least 1. b minus a and c minus b are at least 1, because the elements are increasing integers.

121
00:13:51,408 --> 00:13:53,883
The sum is c, which is at most n.

122
00:13:54,333 --> 00:14:01,458
Last, the inverse: the set b 1, b 1 plus b 2, and b 1 plus b 2 plus b 3.

123
00:14:01,908 --> 00:14:06,367
Problem 3 is worth 8 marks. We count compositions of n.

124
00:14:06,817 --> 00:14:12,406
The number of parts must be 1 mod 5. That is 1, 6, 11, and so on.

125
00:14:12,856 --> 00:14:20,003
Each part must be 2 or 3 mod 5. That is 2, 3, 7, 8, 12, 13, and so on.

126
00:14:20,453 --> 00:14:33,722
For example, 3 plus 2 plus 2 plus 2 plus 2 plus 2 is a composition of 13. It has 6 parts, and 6 is 1 mod 5. Each part is 2 or 3. So it counts.

127
00:14:34,172 --> 00:14:39,847
The composition 13, with one part, also counts. 13 is 3 mod 5.

128
00:14:40,297 --> 00:14:47,913
2 plus 2 plus 2 plus 2 plus 2 does not count. It has 5 parts, and 5 is 0 mod 5.

129
00:14:48,363 --> 00:14:54,720
The composition 4 plus 5 does not count either, because 4 and 5 are not allowed parts.

130
00:14:55,170 --> 00:14:59,522
The answer can be a coefficient of a series with no infinite sum.

131
00:14:59,972 --> 00:15:06,265
Define the set and the weight function, and cite the results from class. Pause the video and try it.

132
00:15:06,715 --> 00:15:17,979
A common mistake is phi of P over 1 minus phi of P, where P is the set of allowed parts. That is the String Lemma, and it allows any number of parts.

133
00:15:18,429 --> 00:15:24,872
Start with one part. Let P be the set of positive integers that are 2 or 3 mod 5.

134
00:15:25,322 --> 00:15:31,637
So P is 2, 3, 7, 8, 12, 13, and so on. The blue cells are in P.

135
00:15:32,087 --> 00:15:34,284
The weight of a part is its value.

136
00:15:34,734 --> 00:15:42,755
So the generating series of P has a 1 at x to the p, for each p in P. All other coefficients are 0.

137
00:15:43,205 --> 00:15:49,840
So phi of P is x squared plus x cubed plus x to the 7 plus x to the 8, and so on.

138
00:15:50,290 --> 00:15:57,607
Group the terms in pairs. Each pair is x squared plus x cubed, times a power of x to the 5.

139
00:15:58,057 --> 00:16:06,079
So phi of P is x squared plus x cubed, times 1 plus x to the 5 plus x to the 10, and so on.

140
00:16:06,529 --> 00:16:18,838
By the geometric series, with y equal to x to the 5, phi of P equals x squared plus x cubed, over 1 minus x to the 5. This has no infinite sum.

141
00:16:19,288 --> 00:16:28,653
A composition with k parts, all in P, is a k-tuple of elements of P. So these compositions form the set P to the k.

142
00:16:29,103 --> 00:16:34,437
The weight of a composition is the sum of its parts. This is the n of the question.

143
00:16:34,887 --> 00:16:41,287
By the Product Lemma, the generating series of P to the k is phi of P, to the power k.

144
00:16:41,737 --> 00:16:51,230
The number of parts must be 1, 6, 11, and so on. That is, k equals 5 j plus 1, for some j at least 0.

145
00:16:51,680 --> 00:17:00,704
Here are compositions with 1 part, 6 parts and 11 parts. They are in P to the 1, P to the 6, and P to the 11.

146
00:17:01,154 --> 00:17:09,133
By the Product Lemma, the series of these sets are phi of P, phi of P to the 6, and phi of P to the 11.

147
00:17:09,583 --> 00:17:19,161
So the set S is the union of the sets P to the 5 j plus 1. They are disjoint, because their elements have different numbers of parts.

148
00:17:19,611 --> 00:17:24,667
By the Sum Lemma, in its infinite form, phi of S is the sum of these series.

149
00:17:25,117 --> 00:17:33,544
Take out one factor phi of P. The rest is 1 plus phi of P to the 5, plus phi of P to the 10, and so on.

150
00:17:33,994 --> 00:17:42,463
Phi of P has constant term 0. So we can use the geometric series, with y equal to phi of P to the 5.

151
00:17:42,913 --> 00:17:47,543
The result is phi of P, over 1 minus phi of P to the 5.

152
00:17:47,993 --> 00:17:57,827
Put the parts together. The number of compositions is the coefficient of x to the n in phi of P, over 1 minus phi of P to the 5.

153
00:17:58,277 --> 00:18:03,845
Here phi of P is x squared plus x cubed, over 1 minus x to the 5.

154
00:18:04,295 --> 00:18:13,362
The official solution clears the inner fractions. Multiply the top and the bottom by 1 minus x to the 5, to the power 5.

155
00:18:13,812 --> 00:18:20,639
The top becomes x squared plus x cubed, times 1 minus x to the 5, to the power 4.

156
00:18:21,089 --> 00:18:29,409
The bottom becomes 1 minus x to the 5, to the power 5, minus x squared plus x cubed, to the power 5.

157
00:18:29,859 --> 00:18:33,869
This gives the second form. Both forms are correct answers.

158
00:18:34,319 --> 00:18:43,877
Check small n. A computer expands the series. The coefficients are 1 at x squared, x cubed, x to the 7 and x to the 8.

159
00:18:44,327 --> 00:18:50,876
At n equal to 12, the coefficient is 2. The two compositions are 12, and six 2s.

160
00:18:51,326 --> 00:18:56,830
At n equal to 13, the coefficient is 7. One composition is 13.

161
00:18:57,280 --> 00:19:04,683
The others have five 2s and one 3. The 3 can be in any of 6 places. So 1 plus 6 gives 7.

162
00:19:05,133 --> 00:19:13,026
At n equal to 10, the coefficient is 0. Five 2s have 5 parts, and 5 is not 1 mod 5.

163
00:19:13,476 --> 00:19:19,620
A brute-force count of all compositions up to n equal to 20 gives the same numbers.

164
00:19:20,070 --> 00:19:27,131
Here is the written solution. Define P and its weight. Find phi of P with the geometric series.

165
00:19:27,581 --> 00:19:34,173
Define the set S of compositions as a union, and give its weight function: the sum of the parts.

166
00:19:34,623 --> 00:19:39,295
Say that the answer is the coefficient of x to the n in phi of S.

167
00:19:39,745 --> 00:19:42,348
Name the Product Lemma for P to the k.

168
00:19:42,798 --> 00:19:46,446
Say why the sets are disjoint. Then name the Sum Lemma.

169
00:19:46,896 --> 00:19:52,208
Then use the geometric series. Say that phi of P has constant term 0.

170
00:19:52,658 --> 00:19:54,343
Last, write the answer.

171
00:19:54,793 --> 00:20:01,663
Problem 4 is worth 5 marks. S sub n is the set of non-empty subsets of 1 to n.

172
00:20:02,113 --> 00:20:06,571
There are two weights. w of X is the smallest element of X.

173
00:20:07,021 --> 00:20:10,051
v of X is the largest element of X.

174
00:20:10,501 --> 00:20:18,053
Part a asks for the generating series of S sub 3, for w and for v. Pause the video and try it.

175
00:20:18,503 --> 00:20:26,033
S sub 3 has 7 subsets. Here they are, one in each row. A blue cell is an element of the subset.

176
00:20:26,483 --> 00:20:28,979
Write the smallest element of each subset.

177
00:20:29,429 --> 00:20:32,757
Four subsets contain 1, so they have weight 1.

178
00:20:33,207 --> 00:20:35,895
Two subsets, 2 and 2 3, have weight 2.

179
00:20:36,345 --> 00:20:38,671
One subset, 3, has weight 3.

180
00:20:39,121 --> 00:20:46,225
So phi of S sub 3, with respect to w, is 4x plus 2x squared plus x cubed.

181
00:20:46,675 --> 00:20:49,811
Now write the largest element of each subset.

182
00:20:50,261 --> 00:20:52,586
Only the subset 1 has weight 1.

183
00:20:53,036 --> 00:20:55,745
Two subsets, 2 and 1 2, have weight 2.

184
00:20:56,195 --> 00:20:59,673
Four subsets contain 3, so they have weight 3.

185
00:21:00,123 --> 00:21:05,584
With respect to v, the series is x plus 2x squared plus 4x cubed.

186
00:21:06,034 --> 00:21:11,538
Part b replaces S sub n with T, the set of all finite subsets of N.

187
00:21:11,988 --> 00:21:20,116
The empty set has no smallest or largest element. So we use the non-empty finite subsets, as in part a.

188
00:21:20,566 --> 00:21:23,638
In the notes, N is 0, 1, 2, and so on.

189
00:21:24,088 --> 00:21:37,443
The generating series is a formal power series only if each weight has finitely many elements. Then the coefficient of x to the n is a number. This is the condition in Definition 2.5.

190
00:21:37,893 --> 00:21:47,642
Take w, the smallest element. The sets 1; 1 2; 1 3; 1 2 3; 1 4; 1 5; and so on, all have weight 1.

191
00:21:48,092 --> 00:21:53,127
There are infinitely many of them. So the coefficient of x is not defined.

192
00:21:53,577 --> 00:21:58,291
So with respect to w, the series is not a formal power series.

193
00:21:58,741 --> 00:22:09,728
Take v, the largest element. If v of X equals m, then X contains m. The other elements form a subset Y of 0 to m minus 1.

194
00:22:10,178 --> 00:22:17,282
There are 2 to the m such subsets. For m equal to 3, there are 2 cubed, which is 8. Here they are.

195
00:22:17,732 --> 00:22:24,729
So each weight has finitely many sets. With respect to v, the series is a formal power series.

196
00:22:25,179 --> 00:22:30,491
Its coefficients are 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, and so on: 2 to the m.

197
00:22:30,941 --> 00:22:36,851
It is the sum of 2 to the m, x to the m, which is 1 over 1 minus 2x.

198
00:22:37,301 --> 00:22:41,823
If N starts at 1, the series is x over 1 minus 2x.

199
00:22:42,273 --> 00:22:50,337
Problem 5 is worth 4 marks. S is the set 0, 1, up to 50. The weight of a is 4 a plus 3.

200
00:22:50,787 --> 00:22:57,998
Find the generating series, and write it as a simplified rational expression. Pause the video and try it.

201
00:22:58,448 --> 00:23:06,320
The element 0 has weight 4 times 0 plus 3, which is 3. So the series has a 1 at x cubed.

202
00:23:06,770 --> 00:23:11,015
The element 1 has weight 4 times 1 plus 3, which is 7.

203
00:23:11,465 --> 00:23:15,967
The element 2 has weight 4 times 2 plus 3, which is 11.

204
00:23:16,417 --> 00:23:18,891
Each step of a adds 4 to the weight.

205
00:23:19,341 --> 00:23:28,003
So the 1s sit at x cubed, x to the 7, x to the 11, and so on, 4 apart. All other coefficients are 0.

206
00:23:28,453 --> 00:23:34,703
The last element, 50, has weight 4 times 50 plus 3, which is 203.

207
00:23:35,153 --> 00:23:40,551
Phi of S is the sum over a from 0 to 50 of x to the 4 a plus 3.

208
00:23:41,001 --> 00:23:45,907
Take out x cubed. The rest is the sum of x to the 4, to the power a.

209
00:23:46,357 --> 00:23:53,120
This is a finite geometric sum. a goes from 0 to 50, so it has 51 terms.

210
00:23:53,570 --> 00:24:03,255
Write phi of S as a row of coefficients. It has a 1 at x cubed, x to the 7, and so on, up to x to the 203.

211
00:24:03,705 --> 00:24:15,247
Multiply phi of S by x to the 4. Each term moves 4 places to the right. The terms now sit at x to the 7, up to x to the 207.

212
00:24:15,697 --> 00:24:25,382
Subtract the second row from the first. Each power from x to the 7 to x to the 203 is in both rows. So it cancels.

213
00:24:25,832 --> 00:24:30,141
Only x cubed and minus x to the 207 remain.

214
00:24:30,591 --> 00:24:38,122
So 1 minus x to the 4, times phi of S, equals x cubed minus x to the 207.

215
00:24:38,572 --> 00:24:41,815
Divide both sides by 1 minus x to the 4.

216
00:24:42,265 --> 00:24:57,283
Take out x cubed. x to the 207 is x cubed times x to the 204. So phi of S equals x cubed, times 1 minus x to the 204, over 1 minus x to the 4.

217
00:24:57,733 --> 00:25:04,197
The exponent 204 is 4 times 51: the step times the number of terms.

218
00:25:04,647 --> 00:25:17,298
A common mistake is x to the 200 or x to the 203 in the numerator. Count the terms: a goes from 0 to 50, so there are 51 terms, not 50.

219
00:25:17,748 --> 00:25:25,300
To recap. Problem 1: count S in two ways, and split the second count by the size of A intersect B.

220
00:25:25,750 --> 00:25:32,406
Problem 2: the bijection takes the gaps between the elements, and the inverse takes partial sums.

221
00:25:32,856 --> 00:25:42,563
Problem 3: the compositions are a disjoint union of powers of P. Use the Product and Sum Lemmas, then the geometric series.

222
00:25:43,013 --> 00:25:47,194
Problem 4: check that each weight has finitely many elements.

223
00:25:47,644 --> 00:25:52,231
Problem 5: a finite geometric sum with 51 terms.

224
00:25:52,681 --> 00:25:56,798
Video 18b continues with problems 6 to 10.
